WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.400
Two structural features decide whether a fatty acid is solid or liquid.

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.300
First, chain length.

3
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:14.320
Among saturated fatty acids, longer chains pack more tightly and melt higher.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:21.740
Lauric acid at 12 carbons melts near 44 degrees, palmitic at 16 near 63, stearic at

5
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.620
18 near 70. Second, saturation.

6
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.560
One cis double bond kinks the chain and breaks the packing.

7
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.820
Stearic acid is a solid.

8
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:37.420
Oleic acid with a single double bond melts at just 13 degrees and pours as an oil.

9
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.180
More double bonds drop it further still.

10
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.380
This is the whole chapter in one graph.

11
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:48.033
A triacylglycerol's melting point depends entirely on which fatty acids hang off the

12
00:00:48.033 --> 00:00:54.033
glycerol. Tripalmitin, three saturated 16-carbon chains, packs tightly and is solid

13
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:55.053
at body temperature.

14
00:00:55.753 --> 00:01:00.793
Triolein, three unsaturated 18-1 chains, stays liquid even at room temperature.

15
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:07.713
A mixed triacylglycerol, one saturated chain and two unsaturated, lands in between,

16
00:01:07.713 --> 00:01:13.153
semi-solid around body temperature, which is the profile of most natural body fat.

17
00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:18.133
Cells fine-tune the physical state of their fat by choosing which fatty acid goes

18
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:19.853
to which position on glycerol.

19
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:25.773
Fatty acid saturation is the main dial a cell turns to tune membrane fluidity.

20
00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:30.793
Saturated tails pack tightly through van der Waals contacts, raising the transition

21
00:01:30.793 --> 00:01:33.193
temperature and pushing the membrane toward a gel.

22
00:01:33.953 --> 00:01:38.933
Unsaturated tails kink and create packing defects, keeping the membrane fluid even

23
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:44.873
when it is cold. The E. coli example is elegant: chilled to 20 degrees, the

24
00:01:44.873 --> 00:01:49.233
bacterium makes more unsaturated fatty acids to hold its fluidity steady.

25
00:01:49.813 --> 00:01:54.693
Tuning composition to keep viscosity constant as the environment changes is

26
00:01:54.693 --> 00:01:56.213
homeoviscous adaptation.

27
00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:00.493
Cholesterol is a fluidity buffer, a thermostat.

28
00:02:00.913 --> 00:02:04.913
At low temperature, it inserts between the tails and disrupts tight packing,

29
00:02:05.253 --> 00:02:06.813
preventing the membrane from gelling.

30
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:11.653
At high temperature, it restrains the same tails, keeping the membrane from becoming

31
00:02:11.653 --> 00:02:16.713
too fluid. On a graph of fluidity against temperature, cholesterol flattens the

32
00:02:16.713 --> 00:02:21.193
sharp phase transition into a broad, gentle curve, giving the cell a stable

33
00:02:21.193 --> 00:02:23.333
operating range across a wide window.

34
00:02:23.873 --> 00:02:26.093
One molecule, two opposite jobs.
